turnkey-redis-19.0 (4) turnkey; urgency=low

  * The overlay no longer ships /etc/apt/sources.list.d/keel.sources
    (apt.keellinux.org, disabled) or /etc/apt/preferences.d/keel (the Keel
    origin at 1001). At 1001 apt downgraded every package newer than the
    archive's (tracker#23), and both files, at the paths common uses, would
    override the source and the 990 pin Keel-Linux/common#30 ships. The
    build time archive still wins over TurnKey's 999 pin during the build:
    conf.d/main pins it by its Label, l=Keel Linux staging, at 1001 before
    the upgrade, and conf.d/zz-project-packages removes that pin with the
    build time source, then fails if any apt source or pin of the image
    still names the build time archive, in place of the check that
    keel.sources was disabled. tests/project-packages.bats (15 tests, 100
    percent) and tests/apt-files.bats check it.

  * The component is pinned at unit-redis v1.0.2, and the recipe's own
    checks are widened to the two defects that pin fixes, both found by
    booting the layer rather than by building it.

    Redis refuses a user declared twice across configuration files, so the
    component no longer publishes an administrative account for the first
    boot to turn on; it declares it once, at the first boot, or not at all.
    conf.d/main checks that exactly one account is declared in the whole of
    /etc/redis and that it is not the administrative one, because a second
    declaration anywhere is a layer whose Redis cannot start.

    And Redis opens every value its logfile setting is ever given, so
    anything that runs the server as root during a build leaves
    /var/log/redis/redis-server.log owned by root in a directory the redis
    user owns, and the service fails to start on every machine built from
    the layer. The component's conf script takes an empty one away, and
    conf.d/main checks that the layer ships none the service could not
    write.

  * README.rst says what the layer is published without, which is now an
    administrative account rather than one that exists and is off.

 -- Keel Linux maintainers <admin@keellinux.org>  Mon, 28 Sep 2026 02:40:00 +0000

turnkey-redis-19.0 (3) turnkey; urgency=low

  * The component is pinned at unit-redis v1.0.1, so the layer manifest
    records "units redis@1.0.1". 1.0.0 read an INFO reply with a grep
    anchored on a dollar, and Redis speaks CRLF: the first build of this
    layer died in the component's own check, with a server that was up and
    listening on both families and a message that said no Redis answered.
    Nothing the image gets changes.

 -- Keel Linux maintainers <admin@keellinux.org>  Mon, 28 Sep 2026 02:00:00 +0000

turnkey-redis-19.0 (2) turnkey; urgency=low

  * Rebuilt as a Keel layer on core, carrying the Redis server as the
    unit.d/redis component (keel-linux/unit-redis), which fab resolves and
    applies and bt-layer records in the layer manifest as
    "units redis@<version>". Decision 0013 is the reason: a database is a
    component, not a parent layer, so every appliance that needs Redis is
    built on this one instead of installing its own.

  * Redis 8.0 from Debian 13 (Trixie), listening on ::1 and 127.0.0.1 only,
    both as literal addresses. Debian ships "bind 127.0.0.1 -::1", where the
    dash makes ::1 optional and a server that cannot bind it starts anyway
    and answers one family. No name is used anywhere: Debian maps ::1 to
    ip6-localhost and never to localhost, which is how the PostgreSQL
    appliance shipped listening on IPv4 only.

  * The Redis secret comes from the instance description and is the password
    of an ACL user, not requirepass. secrets.db_password renders to DB_PASS,
    which firstboot.d/35redispass of the component turns into an ACL rule
    holding the SHA-256 of that password, in a file owned root:redis 0640.
    Two things follow, and both are the point rather than side effects.

    The server answers INFO to anybody who can reach it on loopback and no
    key to anybody without the secret, which is what lets `keel inspect`
    read database.server.role: it never reads a secret to get past a
    refusal, so an appliance with requirepass set could not say that it is
    standalone, nor later that it is a primary or a replica.

    And nothing on the machine can print the secret back, because what the
    configuration holds is a digest. Before the first boot the account is
    published "off", so the layer carries no usable credential at all.

  * Removed with it: 20regen-redis-secrets, which generated a password into
    a world readable /etc/redis/redis.conf; 40redis and bin/redis.py, which
    asked at the console for the bind address and protected mode, both of
    which the instance description and the component now decide;
    turnkey-redis-pw and the confconsole plugin that copied the password
    into /root/redis_password.txt, neither of which has anything to read now
    that the configuration holds a digest.

  * Redis Commander, nginx, the Node.js runtime, pm2 and the landing page
    are deliberately left out: they belong to a web stack, which is the same
    reason Adminer is not in keel-mariadb. If the maintainer wants literal
    parity with the upstream redis appliance, that is a different artefact,
    built on this layer.

  * No Webmin module. Debian 13 packages webmin-mysql and webmin-postgresql
    and no equivalent for Redis. conf.d/main fails the build the day one is
    packaged, and the boot test checks the machine offers none, so the
    absence is a checked fact rather than a silence.

  * The project's own packages (inithooks, confconsole, keel) are installed
    from the build host's archive, verified there with gpgv against the
    staging key rather than trusted, and then checked against what that
    archive offers rather than against a literal version
    (conf.d/zz-project-packages): a literal passes whenever a build reads a
    stale index, which is how the forum layer of 2026-09-26 shipped the
    previous morning's packages with a green build. keel 0.3.5 is the floor,
    because that is where `keel inspect` reads the database section.

  * Tests: 92 bats tests, and a boot test that assembles the published
    layer, boots it in LXC, authenticates over IPv6 with the declared
    secret, writes and reads a key, is refused a key without the secret,
    checks Webmin, reads the role back off the server as standalone, and
    runs keel diff.

 -- Keel Linux maintainers <admin@keellinux.org>  Mon, 28 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000

turnkey-redis-19.0 (1) turnkey; urgency=low

  * Upgrade the base distribution to Debian 13/Trixie.

  * Update Redis to Debian's 8.0 series.

  * Use Debian's Node.js and npm packages for the management application
    runtime.

  * Install Redis Commander from its maintained npm registry release with
    dependency integrity metadata and a supported npm update path.

  * Preserve the selected Redis network binding and reliably reload the
    first-boot Redis Commander credentials.

  * Start Nginx after first-boot TLS certificate generation so Redis Commander
    is available on the initial HTTPS session.

  * See the TurnKey Core 19.0 changelog for changes common to all appliances.

